SELECTION — mechanical, never editorial:
Inclusion is decided ONLY by the rubric, never by taste or by a target count. Include a
clause in risk_and_compliance_insights if and only if its text matches a Critical,
High, or Medium trigger below; if it matches no trigger, omit it silently. Never include a clause
because it "seems important", and never drop a matching clause because the list is
getting long. Report exactly the clauses that match — no more, no less. This OVERRIDES
any "be terse / highlight reel" framing above: for the risk list, rubric completeness
beats brevity.

UNAMBIGUOUS-ONLY — the rule that keeps runs identical:
Report a clause ONLY when its risk is beyond reasonable dispute: the literal text plainly
matches a specific trigger AND any competent reviewer would flag the same clause at the
same tier. If including it is a judgment call, if the tier could be read either way, or if
it is a routine/market term that some reviewers would not flag at all, EXCLUDE it — the
same way, every run. A short list of undeniable risks that is identical on every run beats
a fuller list that shifts between runs. Never include a clause on the strength of a maybe.
Never flag a routine payment, expense, notice, term-length, or boilerplate clause whose
standard terms are present, unless its own text is plainly and severely one-sided. But a
PRESENT clause lacking its own operative term — e.g. a fees clause that states no payment
timing at all — is not routine: flag it as High under the missing-standard-term trigger,
every run. (A stated term that is merely imperfect — e.g. a payment deadline present but
with no late-payment interest — stays unflagged.) This High rule is ONLY for a present
clause missing its term; a standard PROTECTION entirely absent from the contract
(limitation of liability, mutual indemnity) is ALWAYS the Critical trigger above, never
High — the BOUNDARY RULE order decides, the same way every run.

STABILITY — same input, same output:
Inclusion and severity are pure functions of the clause text against the rubric, so a
borderline clause must resolve the SAME way every time. Do not guess on borderline
clauses — apply the BOUNDARY RULE below. List risk items strictly in clause-index order,
top to bottom; never reorder by severity or importance.

STYLE — applies to every field:
Give the fact itself, not a description of it. Never write "the contract states…", 
"it is recommended…", "this clause…".
Never restate a field's own label inside its value.
No N/A padding. No generic compliance filler ("ensure compliance with applicable law").
Phrases, not sentences — except the summary.

SECTIONS:
