key_information: the deal-defining facts; each value the shortest phrase, or "Absent" 
for a missing material term.
timeline_and_key_milestones: only dates or triggers that carry real consequence.
risk_and_compliance_insights: Critical/High/Medium issues worth raising; classify
severity with the rubric below so the same contract always yields the same items.

ACTOR CHECK — mandatory before writing any risk item:
Before writing any issue, identify the grammatical subject of the operative verb — 
who "may," who "shall," who "is required to." State it internally as: "[Party X] holds 
the right/discretion; [Party Y] bears the consequence." Read the literal subject of 
the sentence — do not assume the more common contract pattern. Then write harmed_party 
as [Party Y] and confirm issue is consistent with that party being harmed, not the 
party who initiates or requests.

CANONICALIZATION — one fact, one entry, deterministic anchor:
Each underlying fact gets exactly ONE risk entry anchored to a single clause. If two
issues stem from the same clause family (e.g. two IP-related issues from the same
section), merge them into one entry with a single issue string. When several clauses
impose the SAME specific burden on the same party over the SAME subject matter (e.g. a
use restriction and a confidentiality duty over the same information), anchor ONE entry
to the EARLIEST such clause in the index and cover the rest in its issue. But DISTINCT
obligations are separate facts — a non-compete, an indemnity, an assignment restriction,
a confidentiality duty each get their OWN entry even when they all burden the same
party; never merge entries merely because the burdened party is the same. Never split
one fact across multiple clause_title entries.
MISSING PROTECTIONS — deterministic title choice, apply the same way every run:
- A present clause on the topic is ONE-SIDED or defective (e.g. a warranty/damages
  clause disclaiming only ONE party's liability): report ONE entry on that present
  clause, and state BOTH halves of the fact in its issue (the disclaimer AND that the
  other party's exposure remains uncapped). Do NOT also emit a "Missing:" entry for
  the same topic — one fact, one entry.
- The present clause on the topic is BALANCED and non-defective but the protection is
  still absent (e.g. a mutual consequential-damages waiver exists but NO cap on direct
  damages exists anywhere): use "Missing: <standard name>".
- NO clause touches the topic at all: use "Missing: <standard name>".

RISK & COMPLIANCE — SEVERITY RUBRIC (apply mechanically; the same clause must get 
the same severity on every run):
Test EVERY clause against these triggers. Severity is decided by what the clause text 
says, not by impression. Include an item only if it matches a trigger below; otherwise 
omit it. Do not cap or pad the count — include exactly those that match, in document 
order.

CRITICAL — unrecoverable, unbounded, or irrevocable exposure with no remedy. Flag 
Critical only if the text literally shows one of:
- unlimited or uncapped liability or indemnity falling on one party — an indemnity for
  "any and all claims" / "all losses" with no stated monetary cap counts as uncapped →
  Critical, even if it carries a proportional-fault or negligence carve-out;
- money the PAYING party cannot recover (its prepaid fees forfeited, or advances it
  paid that are never returned to it) — forfeiture of money already paid is ALWAYS
  Critical; "the loss is limited to the amount paid" does NOT make it bounded for this
  purpose, and it is never Medium or High. A clause that merely requires a party to
  RETURN advances it received is a bounded repayment obligation → grade under HIGH,
  not Critical;
- an irrevocable grant of rights, license, or power of attorney (irrevocable =
  automatically Critical, regardless of scope). The word "irrevocable" (or an express
  equivalent) must appear in the clause text — never infer irrevocability from a right
  merely being one-sided or unconditional;
- termination that strips a party of payment already earned or of its only remedy;
- a unilateral right to AMEND the scope, price, or terms of the agreement without the
  other party's written consent — INCLUDING any change-control mechanism under which
  scope or price changes can take effect without BOTH parties' signatures or without
  reflecting all changes and costs (e.g. a revised statement of work that expressly
  requires no signatures or formal execution). This is Critical even when framed as
  discretionary — never Medium. The reverse is equally fixed: a change-control process
  that requires BOTH parties' signatures and reflects all changes and costs is market
  standard — EXCLUDE it entirely, never flag it at any tier. (Amendment/modification
  only; assignment, delegation, or affiliate-transfer rights are NOT covered here —
  grade those under HIGH);
- a standard protection (limitation of liability, mutual indemnity) entirely ABSENT,
  leaving unbounded exposure — always Critical, never High, even when a partial or
  one-category waiver (e.g. consequential-damages-only) exists.

HIGH — materially one-sided, ambiguous, or likely unenforceable, but bounded or 
curable by negotiation:
- a one-sided obligation (indemnity, confidentiality, non-compete, assignment consent,
  IP assignment) binding ONE party only and not mutual, where one party bears the full
  burden and the other bears none. Bright lines — these two match this trigger EVERY
  run; being common does not make them mutual, never exclude them as routine:
  (a) a confidentiality clause that protects only ONE party's information (a one-way
  NDA); (b) an assignment clause that restricts only ONE party (the other party free to
  assign — e.g. to affiliates — or simply unaddressed). A mutual consent requirement or
  mutual restriction stays excluded;
- ambiguous language a court could read against one party;
- a drafting defect that makes a clause's operation uncertain: a cross-reference to
  section numbers that cannot exist in the agreement (e.g. a survival clause citing a
  range beyond the sections the agreement actually has), or internally contradictory
  numbers, words, or ranges within one term (e.g. "thirty (90) days"). A defect like
  this is NEVER "just a typo" — it creates enforceable ambiguity about which
  obligations survive or apply, and matches this trigger EVERY run. A drafting defect
  is ALWAYS High, never Critical — ambiguity about what a clause means is not proven
  unbounded exposure, even in a survival or liability provision;
- a restrictive covenant (non-compete, non-solicit) of doubtful enforceability;
- a missing standard term that leaves a stated obligation INOPERABLE (e.g. a payment
  obligation with NO timing at all, a notice requirement with NO period). If the
  operative term is present (a deadline exists), the clause is complete for this
  trigger — absence of ancillary enhancements (late-payment interest, penalties,
  escalation remedies) is NOT a missing term; never flag it. This trigger NEVER
  applies to an absent standard PROTECTION (limitation of liability, mutual
  indemnity): an absent protection is ALWAYS the Critical trigger above, never High;
- an exclusive governing-law or venue forum in a jurisdiction that is not the stated
  place of business of EITHER party = High (jurisdiction risk), every run; a forum in
  either party's own stated jurisdiction is market standard — exclude it. Apply this
  as a MECHANICAL comparison, never a judgment: read each party's address in the
  preamble, compare with the chosen forum state; if the forum matches neither address,
  flag High — do not weigh convenience, neutrality, or reasonableness.

MEDIUM — bounded commercial friction: a real, negotiable disadvantage that cannot cause
unbounded or unrecoverable harm. GUARD: if a discretionary right can change the
agreement's own scope, price, or terms, that is the Critical AMEND trigger above, never
Medium — Medium discretion covers only operational rights (support, charges, approvals)
that leave the agreement's terms intact. Flag Medium ONLY on these two triggers:
- a right exercisable at one party's SOLE discretion (the word "sole" or an express
  equivalent must appear) that lets that party impose charges, delays, or approval
  gates on the other, bounded in scope. A right qualified by a reasonableness standard
  ("reasonable satisfaction", "not unreasonably withheld") does NOT match — exclude it;
- a stated constraint on one party's pricing or business freedom that is bounded (e.g.
  a most-favored-pricing obligation requiring one party to give its best terms).

EXCLUDE — never flag balanced or mutual clauses, market-standard terms, or boilerplate
with no asymmetric risk. Do NOT flag routine settlement mechanics both parties expect —
a return or true-up of unearned advances, prepaid amounts, or materials on termination —
unless the text creates a genuine forfeiture or a one-sided, unrecoverable loss.

BOUNDARY RULE — deterministic tie-break, never a judgment call:
Test the clause against the Critical triggers FIRST. If it matches ANY Critical trigger,
it is Critical. Otherwise, if it matches ANY High trigger, it is High. Otherwise, if it
matches ANY Medium trigger, it is Medium. Otherwise omit it. NEVER output Low — a clause
that would only grade Low is omitted entirely. Apply this exact order every time, so a
borderline clause can never flip severity or drop between runs.

